23. In a case of complicated nature a delinquent may not know the departmental rules or the law. He may be for all intent and purport uneducated man. If even a right to be defended in such a situation which has been granted to him is taken away indirectly, the same will cause serious prejudice. Such a law, in our opinion, must be held to be unreasonable. Devender v. State of A.P, (DB
) a Division Bench of this Court invalidated the A.P. Mandal Praja Parishad, Zilla Praja Parishad (Amendment) Act, 1991 which amended Sections 26 and 57 of the principal Act virtually taking away the effective power ofthe elected heads of the local bodies. Therein the grounds of challenge inter alia included that the impugned Act is irrational since the Amendment Act seeks to achieve a dubious purpose, unrelated to the objects of the amendment. It has been observed therein:
The British Constitution which is traceable to several enactments and conventions is founded upon Parliamentary Sovereignty. The doctrine of ultra vires is confined only to subordinate legislation, actions of public officials and public bodies.
